  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07140825/TMUA-2019-paper-1.pdf#page=21\">TMUA 2019 · Paper 1 · Question 19</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07140826/TMUA-2019-paper-1-worked-answers.pdf#page=22\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2019 · Paper 1 · Question 19",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2019 · Paper 1 · Question 19 — Evaluate the sum of sin(10+90k) degrees for k=0,...,90.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Look for angular repetition",
      "body": "Write the first few sine terms, reducing their angles using the full-turn period. Continue far enough to find a block that repeats.",
      "recap": "Sine repeats after a full turn, and a half-turn changes its sign.",
      "pitfall": "The input is in degrees and the index starts at zero.",
      "pause": "Identify a repeating block and calculate its sum.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "Sine repeats after a full turn, and a half-turn changes its sign."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Split into blocks and a remainder",
      "body": "Count the terms using both inclusive endpoints. Divide that count by your block length and retain the partial block at the end.",
      "recap": "A periodic sum consists of complete blocks followed by a remainder.",
      "pitfall": "A vanishing full block does not permit discarding a partial block.",
      "pause": "Find the number of full blocks and the remainder yourself, then sum the surviving terms.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "A periodic sum consists of complete blocks followed by a remainder."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>sin</mi><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>sin</mi><msup><mn>100</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The terms repeat <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> because adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>180</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> changes the sign of sine and adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> repeats it. Each four-term block sums to zero.</p><p>There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>91</mn></mrow></math> terms, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>91</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>22</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Thus twenty-two full blocks cancel, leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer C: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><msup><mn>100</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02218;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</strong></p>",
